  "post": {
    "caption": "Ponte Vecchio once sold meat. Ponte Vecchio means Old Bridge, and Florence rebuilt it in 1345. Its shops first held butchers and tanners. In 1565, Duke Cosimo had Vasari build a private corridor above them. Vasari built it in five months, for the duke's son's wedding. That corridor is about one kilometer long and passes over the shops. The smell from the butchers' stalls drifted up into the passage. So Grand Duke Ferdinando moved jewelers into the butchers' place. Goldsmiths and jewelers still sell from those same shops today. Their shops have stood there since the 1590s.",
